## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of conditional Terraform blocks, ensuring safe incremental changes and plan stability when toggling a feature flag.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic blocks in Terraform
- Conditional resource configuration
- AWS S3 server-side encryption basics
- Plan drift and backward compatibility

## Code Example
```javascript
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "b" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  dynamic "server_side_encryption_configuration" {
    for_each = var.enable_sse ? [1] : []
    content {
      rule {
        apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
          sse_algorithm = "AES256"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support SSE with a customer-managed key (KMS) via kms_master_key_id?
- What tests would you add to validate idempotency when toggling the flag across environments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-env isolation, simple gating, and multi-provider setups for real-world tools like Databricks and Snowflake in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Env tagging across modules
- Per-env remote state backends
- Provider aliases for multi-environment isolation
- Lightweight CI gates for plan validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend snippet (per-env)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state-bucket"
    key    = "env/dev/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tf-locks-dev"
  }
}

// wrapper module outline
module "env_wrapper" {
  source = "./modules/env-wrapper"
  environment = var.environment
  tags = { Environment = var.environment }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend gates for Snowflake/DATABRICKS resources?
- How would you test the gating logic locally before CI?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-cloud module design, provider aliasing, and policy-driven governance at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform multi-provider modules
- Provider aliasing and per-tenant scoping
- Open Policy Agent integration with terraform show -json plan
- CI gates and fail-fast

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module interface skeleton (Terraform-like)
module data_lake {
  source = ./modules/data_lake
  tenants = {
    t1 = { cloud = aws, region = us-east-1 }
    t2 = { cloud = gcp, region = us-central1 }
  }
  quotas = {
    t1 = { data_lake_size_gb = 100 }
    t2 = { data_lake_size_gb = 200 }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Sample OPA policy skeleton
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.resource_changes[_].change.actions[_] == create
}
```

